Statement by General Zaw Min Tun, Head of the State Planning and Administration Council Press Office, regarding the situation of the clashes in Shan State (Northeastern) and Rakhine State on 25-11-2023

Joint Statement on the Impact of Floods Across the Country
September 17, 2024
1. In early September 2024, the intensity of Typhoon Ragi, a very powerful cyclone that originated in the South China Sea, caused widespread rainfall, flooding and landslides in northern Thailand and in Myanmar, including Shan State, Karenni State, Chin State, Kayin State, Mandalay Region, Bago Region, Sagaing Region and Magway Region, causing damage and loss. 
2. Thousands of people are still missing and dead in Mandalay Region, Shan State, Karenni State and Bago Region due to heavy rains and the release of water from dams over the past three days. The flood disaster is estimated to have affected about (700,000) people, causing the collapse of houses, loss of property, damage to roads, bridges and infrastructure, flooding of agricultural land, the death of livestock and the destruction of water resources. 
3. In response to such damage, the Government of National Unity, the Ministry of Humanitarian Affairs and Disaster Management (MoHADM), the ethnic resistance groups, the Karen National Union (KNU), the Karenni National Progressive Party (KNPP), and the Chin National Front (CNF), are making every effort to provide relief and emergency response to the flood-affected people using available resources. 
4. Due to the intensity of the storm and the severity of the flood, there is a great need for assistance in providing food, drinking water, consumer goods, and shelter, as well as for health care, water and environmental sanitation, agriculture, and livestock, and for the reconstruction of damaged houses and infrastructure. 
5. Therefore, we call on the UN, EU, ASEAN, and local and international organizations involved in humanitarian assistance to:
(a) Ensure that emergency humanitarian assistance and health assistance are equally available to the flood-affected people;
(b) Directly provide such assistance to the relevant ministries of humanitarian affairs, ethnic resistance groups, and civil society organizations in the respective regions;
(c) Special care is taken to ensure that international humanitarian assistance is not exploited for political and military purposes;
(d) Provide short-term and long-term support not only for relief efforts but also for rehabilitation and reconstruction issues for the flood-affected people;
(e) Encourage international donations to help the people of Myanmar, who are facing war and natural disasters at the same time despite global and limited funding;
(f) Transparently report on donations to the flood-affected people;
(g) Support and provide assistance that includes the needs of vulnerable groups, while adhering to the humanitarian imperative;
(h) Effectively manage and systematically monitor aid without wasting it.
6. We stand in solidarity with all ethnic people affected by the floods caused by Cyclone Ragi and share their feelings for the difficulties they are facing. We also acknowledge and express our sincere gratitude to the social organizations and volunteers who are providing relief and relief work to the flood-affected people. We reaffirm that all our organizations will work together to rebuild the flood-affected areas. 
