Statement by Ambassador Kyaw Moe Tun, Permanent Representative of the Republic of the Union of Myanmar to the United Nations, at the High-Level Plenary Meeting to Commemorate and Promote the International Day for the Total Elimination of Nuclear Weapons, held on 26 September 2024

[Below Myanmar Text]

Press Release

At the High-Level Plenary Meeting to Commemorate and Promote the International Day for the Total Elimination of Nuclear Weapons, Ambassador Kyaw Moe Tun, Permanent Representative of the Republic of the Union of Myanmar to the United Nations, appealed to all Member States not to transfer weapons, ammunition and related materials, including jet fuel, to the Myanmar military in order to stop the brutal repression of the Burmese people by the illegal coup d’état (26 September 2024)

1. The High-Level Plenary Meeting of the United Nations General Assembly to Commemorate and Promote the International Day for the Total Elimination of Nuclear Weapons was held at the United Nations Headquarters in New York on 26 September 2024.

2. The meeting was attended by Ambassador U Kyaw Moe Htun, Permanent Representative of Myanmar to the United Nations. The key points of the speech delivered by the Permanent Representative of Myanmar were as follows:

(a) Today is the International Day for the Total Elimination of Nuclear Weapons, at a time when the world is facing unprecedented political conflicts, geopolitical tensions, the deterioration of international security architecture and the frequent use of the word nuclear weapons;

(b) We call on nuclear-weapon states to act in accordance with international legal norms for disarmament, as do other states; and we are disappointed that the Review Conference of the Parties to the Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons (NPT) has failed to reach an agreement on the meeting for two consecutive times; A new international approach is needed to ensure a world free of nuclear weapons for future generations;

(c) Myanmar condemns any form of threat by nuclear-weapon States against non-nuclear-weapon States; Any use of nuclear weapons would be a violation of the Charter of the United Nations and would have grave humanitarian and environmental consequences; the only way to avoid such threats is through the complete elimination of nuclear weapons;

(d) Myanmar reaffirms its full commitment to the complete elimination of nuclear weapons; and that Myanmar has signed the Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons (TPNW) under its elected civilian government; The United Nations has ratified the Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty (CTBT), but the illegal coup in 2021 has put the ratification process of the TPNW on hold. Despite these challenges, Myanmar will continue to adhere to all the goals of establishing a world free of nuclear weapons, including its participation in the CTBT Declaration issued during the High-Level Discussion of the 79th Session of the United Nations General Assembly;

(e) Nuclear weapons and weapons of mass destruction pose a grave threat to the world, as they are capable of indiscriminate and indiscriminate destruction; The Burmese people have been suffering from the use of heavy weapons and air strikes since the illegal military coup in 2021, and are well aware of the destructive power of these weapons; and

(d) Since the attempted coup in February 2021, the military has systematically and widely committed atrocities against civilians, including indiscriminate aerial bombings, destruction and burning of villages and public property;

(e) The entire Burmese population, especially women and girls, children, the disabled and youth, have borne the brunt of the brutal military regime and the brutality of the military;

(f) Since the coup, more than 5,700 people have been brutally killed by the military, more than 3.4 million have been displaced, and more than 18.6 million are in urgent need of humanitarian assistance;

(g) More than half of those in need of humanitarian assistance are women and girls;

(h) They suffer disproportionately from unemployment and the lack of basic services needed to respond to, prevent and document violence against them due to displacement, including gender-based violence, rape, sexual exploitation and abuse, and from security threats, destruction of infrastructure and gender-based crimes such as trafficking;

(i) Despite these challenges, women are at the forefront of the fight against military rule and the restoration of democracy in Myanmar;

(j) On the other hand, more than 6 million children in Myanmar struggle with displacement, lack of access to health care and regular education, and in some cases, children are forced to study in makeshift camps and bomb shelters due to military airstrikes; Similarly, they are facing malnutrition due to inadequate food and regular vaccinations, and even underage children are at risk of forced recruitment by the military.

(k) On the other hand, people with disabilities in Myanmar, especially women with disabilities, are suffering the most, with accessibility to support measures becoming more difficult and rights violations increasing, and women and girls, including children, are losing limbs due to military atrocities, aerial bombings and landmines.

(l) Similarly, the youth of our country are playing a very important leadership role in the struggle for democracy. Instead of studying in schools and universities, most of them are involved in the people’s movement to save their lives and communities from the brutal military, while some have fled the country to escape arbitrary arrest by the military. The problem of mass displacement and brain drain due to the military’s illegal recruitment has become a matter of grave concern.

(m) The recent cyclone Ragi and its aftermath have further exacerbated the displacement, suffering and humanitarian situation of the people.

(n) Most countries have reported to this Committee on their efforts to provide social protection for their citizens, and in Myanmar, instead of focusing on social protection, there is a loud demand to help protect the people from the brutal military aggression.

