Statement by Ambassador Kyaw Moe Tun, Permanent Representative of the Republic of the Union of Myanmar to the United Nations, at General Discussion on Advancement of Women of the Third Committee of the 79th session of UN General Assembly, held on 16 October 2024

[Below Myanmar Text]

Press Release

At the General Discussion on Advancement of Women of the Third Committee of the 79th session of UN General Assembly, held on 16 October 2024

[Below Myanmar Text]

Ambassador Kyaw Moe Tun, Permanent Representative of the Republic of Myanmar to the United Nations, said

The military is systematically targeting women and using sexual violence as a weapon of war to intimidate and intimidate them. Despite the horrific and dire circumstances faced by brave and selfless women and girls, including women with disabilities, women human rights defenders in Myanmar are at the forefront of resisting the military, ending the military dictatorship and establishing a federal democratic system.

We are grateful to them, We would like to pay tribute to their courage and self-sacrifice in resisting the military dictatorship.

The National Unity Government, ethnic revolutionary organizations, civil society organizations, and especially women’s human rights defenders are continuously working to hold the terrorist army accountable for the crimes they have committed (New York, October 16, 2024)

1. The (79th) session of the United Nations General Assembly, Social, Humanitarian and Cultural Affairs Committee (Third Committee), held a General Discussion on the Development of Women on 8-10-2024, 9-10-2024 and 16-10-2024. Ambassador U Kyaw Moe Htun, Permanent Representative of Myanmar to the United Nations, delivered a speech at the meeting held on 16-10-2024.

2. The key points of the speech delivered by the Permanent Representative of Myanmar were as follows:

(a) The international community has reached the halfway point in its efforts to implement the 2030 Agenda, with the successful adoption of the “Contract for the Future” last month;

(b) However, there is still a long way to go to achieve Sustainable Development Goal (5) on gender equality and the empowerment of women and girls;

(c) While it is widely recognized that gender equality and the empowerment of women and girls benefit communities, the increasing number of conflicts in various regions continue to threaten the rights of women and girls, particularly the exclusion of women from peace and security processes and the continued impunity for violence against them;

(d) Myanmar is on track to achieve the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development by 2021. Since the attempted coup in February, the armed forces have systematically and widely committed acts of sexual violence, including rape, in extremely brutal and inhumane ways;

(e) According to AAPP data, 1,132 women have been killed and 5,649 have been detained; and the majority of the 18.6 million people in need of humanitarian assistance are women and girls, including women with disabilities;

(f) In addition to this dire humanitarian situation, women and girls continue to suffer disproportionately from gender-based violence in conflict-affected areas;

Welcoming the Court’s decision as an important step towards a thorough investigation into the serious crimes committed, we have formally communicated to the International Court of Justice (ICJ) that we accept the Court’s jurisdiction and withdraw all previous preliminary objections, and we are regularly submitting the necessary reports in accordance with the Court’s decision.

(d) We would like to emphasize that any decision of the International Court of Justice, a principal organ of the United Nations, regarding the representation of Myanmar in the Court must be in line with the decision of the General Assembly. Any contradiction would raise questions about the proper functioning of the entire United Nations system.

(e) From the perspective of international law, the International Court of Justice, the principal judicial organ of the United Nations, has not accepted the representation of the Counter-Terrorism Council. It is necessary to be systematically consistent with the position of the United Nations, which does not recognize the legitimacy of the military regime.

(f) Furthermore, the fundamental principle of international law, ex injuria jus non oritur (no legal rights can be claimed on the basis of unlawful acts), clearly states that illegal takeovers do not have the right to claim any legal rights from their illegal actions, including the right to represent the State before international courts.

(g) This principle is also included in the Security Council resolution S/RES/2669 (2022), in which the UN Security Council explicitly condemned the actions of the military regime and the systematic human rights violations.

(h) It is necessary to emphasize that the member states should be aware of the recent atrocities committed by the military regime in Butalin Township, Sagaing Region. In a pre-planned act of violence, the military regime killed (3) children, (i) The killing of 24 innocent civilians, including (3) women and (1) elderly person, was brutally reported;

(i) In addition to the simple killing of innocent civilians, the brutal dismemberment of their bodies and their deliberate display in public places were not only a violation of international law, but also a fundamental assault on the fundamental principles of human dignity and humanity;

(j) These unethical and immoral acts demonstrate a deliberate strategy designed to inflict the greatest psychological trauma on communities, and indicate an unimaginable departure from basic human ethics and values; The deliberate targeting and premeditated atrocities against innocent civilians, including children, were designed not only to kill people but also to inflict collective trauma that will be felt for generations to come; 

(k) Such heinous acts go beyond the level of war crimes and constitute a grave attack on the cultural fabric of the entire human race; and the International Court of Justice needs to consider the risk that by allowing perpetrators of such heinous crimes to appear in its proceedings, it could undermine the principles of international justice and human dignity that the Court itself is responsible for upholding;

Intervention by Ambassador Kyaw Moe Tun, Permanent Representative of the Republic of the Union of Myanmar to the United Nations, at Interactive Dialogue with the High Commissioner for Refugees at the Third Committee of the 79th Session of the UN General Assembly, held on 6 November 2024

[Below Myanmar Text]

Press Release

In an interactive dialogue with the Office of the High Commissioner for Refugees at the Third Committee of the 79th Session of the UN General Assembly, Ambassador Kyaw Moe Tun, Permanent Representative of the Republic of the Union of Myanmar to the United Nations, said that many Burmese people, including the Rohingya, have been forced to flee their homes to escape the brutal airstrikes, arbitrary arrests, violence and forced recruitment by the military junta, and have sought refuge in remote areas or in neighboring and other regional countries.

Refugees trapped in neighboring countries face numerous security challenges and other uncertainties, including regarding resettlement opportunities. They are also facing uncertainty, they have approached UNHCR and are trying to obtain international protection, many of them are waiting for a response from UNHCR, they are traumatized and feel helpless,

The High Commissioner for Refugees said that he would like to ask what kind of message of encouragement and support he can give to these Burmese people trapped in neighboring countries, for their safety, health and the future of themselves and their children (New York, 6 November 2024)

1. The Social, Humanitarian and Cultural Affairs Committee (Third Committee) held an interactive session with the Office of the United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees on Wednesday afternoon, 6 November 2024, during the 79th session of the United Nations General Assembly, currently held at United Nations Headquarters in New York.

2. The discussion was led by the United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees (UNHCR), Mr. Filippo Grandi, who presented the UNHCR report, and was joined by UN Member States, including Ambassador U Kyaw Moe Htun, the Permanent Representative of Myanmar to the UN.

3. The following were the main points raised by the Permanent Representative of Myanmar:

(a) The report submitted by UNHCR today is truly alarming;

(b) The report provides a stark and stark picture of the ongoing refugee crisis caused by the military coup that took place in Myanmar on 1-2-2021 and the brutal and indiscriminate attacks that continue to be perpetrated against civilians throughout the country, in violation of human rights, war crimes and crimes against humanity;

(c) The military council’s brutal airstrikes, arbitrary arrests, Many Burmese people, including the Rohingya, have been forced to flee their homes to escape violence and forced recruitment, seeking refuge in remote areas or in neighboring and other regional countries;

(d) Most refugees, who come from diverse social backgrounds, have sought political asylum and temporary protection in third countries;

(e) We would like to express our gratitude to the governments and peoples of host countries for their assistance to our Burmese people in distress, and call on them to provide them with the necessary assistance and care;

(d) At the same time, the Rohingya continue to be vulnerable to human rights and humanitarian problems, as well as to human rights violations under the military regime’s forced recruitment, and that their suffering has been exacerbated since the coup d’état, and that the large influx of refugees has affected Bangladesh and other regional countries; 

(e) Whereas the Government of National Unity announced its position on the Rohingya issue in Rakhine State on 3-6-2021, and also welcomed the Court’s decision to hold a detailed hearing on the case against Myanmar for violations of the provisions of the Convention on the Prevention and Punishment of Genocide, as an important step towards conducting a thorough investigation into the crimes committed against the Rohingya in military operations in 2016 and 2017; That the Court has been approached through formal channels since the beginning of time for such acceptance and the withdrawal of the initial objections previously submitted, 

(f) It is therefore of particular importance that there be continued consistency in the UN system with regard to the representation of Myanmar in the Court, and that any deviation from the fundamental principle of international law, which expressly prohibits those who have seized power by force of law from being able to represent their State before the Court for their illegal actions, including the right to represent their State before the Court, could have unintended and dangerous consequences, 

(g) In the plenary session of the UN General Assembly held on 29-10-2024, during the discussion on the report of the International Court of Justice, it was emphasized that any decision taken by the International Court of Justice, which is a principal organ of the United Nations, must be consistent with the decisions of the General Assembly, and that any discrepancy would only call into question the consistency of the UN system, 

(h) Here, I would like to revisit the three common tests of legitimacy in international legal practice:

(1) Territorial control: The military regime controls less than half of Myanmar’s territory and is losing ground to the opposition forces on a daily basis;

(2) Consent of the Burmese people: The military regime has absolutely no consent or obedience from the Burmese people, a fact that was evident early in the coup and is now becoming even more apparent;

(3) Compliance with international law and treaties: The military regime violates international law on a daily basis, and also flouts the legal norms emanating from The Hague, where the International Court of Justice, the International Criminal Court and the Organisation for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons are based. For example, the military regime is using chemical weapons against its own people, a fact that I have raised at the UN General Assembly. It is with genuine concern for our people that we reiterate our hope that the representatives, activists and concerned citizens who are present at this meeting will speak out about the horrific events taking place in Myanmar.

(i) The International Court of Justice must therefore reconsider the risk that allowing perpetrators of atrocities to be tried in its proceedings could undermine the most fundamental principles of international justice and human dignity that the Court is mandated to protect.

(j) The Government of National Unity, ethnic resistance organizations, civil society organizations, human rights defenders and the people have a strong determination to continue working towards their ultimate goal despite these challenges, and the Rohingya issue is also part of the current crisis, and therefore, it is necessary to work together with all to ensure that the Rohingya refugees can return to their homes safely, voluntarily and with dignity. 

(k) We thank the host governments and communities for sharing the burden and accepting them. On the other hand, we call on governments and partners to provide support to humanitarian workers to ensure that vulnerable communities can access essential and appropriate humanitarian assistance without hindrance, due to insufficient funding. 

(l) Similarly, in order to fully and effectively resolve the Myanmar crisis, the UN Security Council and other relevant authorities should adopt a coordinated and coherent approach that is in line with the aspirations of the Burmese people; 

(m) The Security Council has seen no progress in the implementation of Council resolution 2669 by the military junta; therefore, the Security Council should make good use of the resolution on Myanmar adopted by the Human Rights Council this year to effectively exercise its mandate; and, furthermore, there has been no progress in implementing the five ASEAN Common Understandings;

(n) UN member states should also act in accordance with their words and commitments;

(o) In particular, the military junta should stop providing arms, ammunition, Countries that provide materials such as jet fuel and money must also cease their support, recognizing that their actions are perpetuating the military regime and allowing it to commit even more atrocities against its people; 

(a) Some countries are giving space to the sham elections being prepared by the military council, which is a highly irrational act; 

(h) The military council has no moral or legal authority to hold elections, and any elections held under its authority will not be free, fair and inclusive elections based on democratic standards, but will only prolong the conflict and the suffering of the people; 

(r) Our people categorically reject such sham elections, and we call on all member states and individuals to do the same, based on the will and aspirations of their people; 

(d) He did not deny that the Myanmar issue was complex, but that this should not be an obstacle to the international community resolving its problems. He believed that if we work together, we could fully and successfully resolve the Myanmar issue, including the Rohingya crisis.

(e) The Government of National Unity has consistently shown its acceptance of the International Court of Justice’s ruling and its readiness to cooperate with relevant parties as necessary. He also supported the international position of a Myanmar-led approach to the Myanmar issue and a regional solution. However, he stressed that these actions must be based on the aspirations and interests of the Burmese people.

(P) However, we urgently need the assistance of the international community, especially the United Nations and the Security Council, which are responsible for maintaining international peace and security.

(F) We therefore urge the international community to assist us in our efforts to end the junta’s impunity, to eradicate the military dictatorship and to establish a federal democratic union in the following ways: 

(1) To pay special attention to the situation in Burma; 

(2) To oppose the sham elections being prepared by the junta and not to grant any legitimacy to the junta; to reject all representation of the junta at bilateral meetings, regional and international meetings and conferences; 

(3) To impose targeted and coordinated sanctions on the junta and its affiliates, and to prevent any arms transfers, aviation fuel and financial support to them;

(4) ASEAN to work effectively and decisively to protect civilians and support the people in Myanmar, and to implement the five ASEAN Common Understandings;

(5) The Security Council to use its existing mandate effectively to address the root causes of the problem, end impunity and hold the military council responsible for crimes accountable;

(6) The Security Council to strengthen the roles of the General Assembly and the Human Rights Council to enable it to carry out its responsibilities effectively and in a timely manner;

(7) The Security Council to refer the entire Myanmar case to the International Criminal Court without delay, in accordance with the recommendations of the High Commissioner for Human Rights and other relevant persons with authority over Myanmar;

(b) In particular, we call on the international community to take more compassionate, humane and effective measures in a timely manner to prevent the continued atrocities committed by the military regime against innocent civilians, to save lives and to ensure a better and sustainable future for the people and future generations. 

(b) Justice must be ensured in Myanmar and elsewhere.

4. The full text of the speech delivered by the Permanent Representative of Myanmar (in English) is attached.

2024, November 11th 

 Statement by Ambassador Kyaw Moe Tun, Permanent Representative of the Republic of the Union of Myanmar to the United Nations, at the event : "Rohingya in Crisis 2017-2024 – Violence, Broken promises and a future for justice" hosted by Irrawaddy Policy Exchange (IEP) (Virtually - The Hague, 11 November 2024)

 Good afternoon, Excellencies, distinguished guests, ladies and gentlemen.

 It is my great honor to participate virtually in this event in the presence of Minister Daw Zin Mar Aung, who is in person, and Deputy Minister U Aung Kyaw Moe, who is online, of the National Unity Government.

 I thank the Irrawaddy Policy Exchange (IPE) and the co-hosts for organizing this event and everyone present here to exchange views on an important topic which needs to be adequately addressed and to have sustained attention from the international community.

 My remarks will focus on the international aspect of the crisis in Myanmar.

 Distinguish guests,

 Today, I regret to inform you that the turmoil in Myanmar continues to worsen since the attempted illegal military coup on 1 February 2021. In almost 4 years, the military junta has been carrying out enormous atrocities that are amounting to war crimes and crimes against humanity.

(c) The draft resolution is directly relevant to Myanmar and the people of Myanmar and should have been widely discussed with our delegation throughout the negotiation process, based on the principle of state ownership and state leadership;

(d) However, we are grateful to the delegations of Bangladesh, Saudi Arabia and the European Union for having consulted with us in certain circumstances and for having accepted certain points of concern from our side; 

(e) However, we are deeply disappointed that the draft resolution has not been able to include other important texts, including those related to the cessation of arms and jet fuel exports to the military regime; 

(f) In particular, such texts are not new and were already included in resolution 55/20, which was adopted unanimously by the Human Rights Council; 

(g) That the text has not been included in the draft resolution is extremely disappointing, and that this situation reflects a lack of balance within the United Nations system. 

(h) That the delegations have repeatedly called for the text to be included in the draft resolution. 

(i) That the matter is of great importance to the lives of our people, especially those on the ground, and that it is imperative to stress that the situation on the ground is far worse than we can imagine. 

(j) That the military regime, in a situation that has created a lack of internal law and order, has carried out indiscriminate air strikes and bombings on civilians, arson attacks on villages, mass killings, arbitrary arrests and detentions, The Burmese people are suffering on an unprecedented scale due to the ongoing torture and other forms of violence, including sexual and gender-based violence, forced recruitment, brutality and the encouragement of cross-border crimes;

(k) It is clear that the military regime is being allowed to commit such inhumane crimes due to the direct and indirect support it receives from all corners of the international community;

(l) The atrocities committed by the military regime, in particular, have increased significantly following recent reciprocal visits abroad;

(m) Furthermore, the military council has carried out a series of airstrikes in southern Kachin State and northern Shan State over the past week, resulting in the deaths of many civilians, including children;

(n) Therefore, the military regime must be provided with arms, ammunition and We urge the UN Member States and the international community, in humanitarian terms, to immediately stop the flow of dual-use items, jet fuel and financial support to Myanmar, 

(o) The failure to adequately address and address the crisis in Myanmar raises the gravest questions: are the international community, and in particular the UN Member States, turning a blind eye to the plight of innocent civilians in Myanmar and supporting the heinous intentions of the military regime that is committing crimes?

(d) The military coup not only exacerbates the setbacks to the promotion and protection of women and girls’ rights, but also exacerbates the violence against them and undermines gender equality; 

(e) At the same time, the military and its affiliates continue to commit indiscriminate airstrikes and bombings, the burning of villages, arbitrary arrests, torture, forced conscription, discrimination, exploitation, rape, the use of sexual and gender-based violence as a weapon of war, and war crimes and crimes against humanity, making women, girls, children and LGBTQ groups particularly vulnerable; 

(f) They bear the brunt of the military’s brutal and inhumane atrocities; 

(g) However, the courageous Burmese women human rights defenders continue to play a vital role in the struggle against the military regime, and no threat from the military regime can deter them from their efforts to protect and promote the rights of vulnerable groups and to fight for the end of the military regime; 

(h) It is therefore essential that we, and the international community as a whole, work together to provide technical assistance and the necessary resources, including easily manageable financial resources, to protect the safety of women and girls and to enable them to participate equally, effectively and meaningfully in all sectors, and to empower women and protect their rights, in all these activities; 

(i) Myanmar, while facing the military regime, They are firmly committed to ending their arbitrary military rule and impunity and establishing a federal democratic union; 

(j) The Government of National Unity, in partnership with ethnic revolutionary organizations, civil society organizations, non-governmental organizations and women’s human rights defenders, will continue to uphold and implement its commitment to combat all forms of violence against women and girls; 

(k) The Government of National Unity is launching a “16 Days of Activism” campaign from today as an effort to end violence against women and girls; 

(l) Therefore, the international community must not leave Myanmar out of the equation in its efforts to end violence against women and girls; 

(m) Address the root causes of the problem and end impunity; We reiterate our call for the United Nations to continue to lead and coordinate collective action to hold perpetrators accountable and to bring justice to victims and survivors.

5. The full text of the statement by the Permanent Representative of Myanmar is attached (in English).

25 November 2024
Permanent Mission of Myanmar to the United Nations, New York

