Statement by Ambassador Kyaw Moe Tun, Permanent Representative of the Republic of the Union of Myanmar to the United Nations, at the High-level meeting to mark the twenty-fifth anniversary of the International Day for the Elimination of Violence against Women at the 79th session of the United Nations General Assembly (New York, 25 November 2024)

Mr. President,

Remarks by Ambassador Kyaw Moe Tun, Permanent Representative of the Republic of the Union of Myanmar to the United Nations, at the UN Security Council media stakeout, held on 30 January 2025

[Below Myanmar Text]

Press release

In a briefing to the media on the Myanmar issue by the Security Council, Ambassador Kyaw Moe Tun, Permanent Representative of the Republic of the Union of Myanmar to the United Nations, said that the people of Myanmar are deeply disappointed with the lack of effective action by the international community, especially the Security Council,

The people of Myanmar will continue to work hard to eradicate the military dictatorship, and the people are determined to build a new Myanmar with a federal democratic union,

To oppose the sham elections being planned by the military in order to save the lives and future of the people of Myanmar, and to stop the flow of weapons to the military, He reiterated his call on the United Nations and the international community to stop the flow of jet fuel and civilian-military equipment to the military (New York, 30 January 2025)

1. The UN Security Council held a press briefing on the situation in Myanmar on 30-1-2025 (Thursday), at 1445 hours, during which statements were made by the Chargé d’Affaires ai/Deputy Permanent Representative of the United Kingdom to the United Nations, a permanent member of the Security Council and Penholder for Myanmar, and the Permanent Representative of Myanmar to the United Nations, Ambassador U Kyaw Moe Htun.

2. The statement by the Permanent Representative of Myanmar included the following key points:

(a) In the next (2) days, it will be (4) years since the illegal military coup in Myanmar;

(b) Every day in the past (4) years has been a tragic day for the people of Myanmar; (6,230) innocent civilians have been killed by the military, over 3.5 million people have been displaced, and an estimated (20) million people are in urgent need of humanitarian assistance;

(c) The people of Burma live in constant fear and no one feels safe;

(d) Despite these dire circumstances, the military continues to commit atrocities, crimes against humanity, crimes against humanity and war crimes;

(e) I and the entire Burmese people have repeatedly called on the international community, including the United Nations, to come to our aid in this time of need;

(f) Initially, the people deeply believed that the United Nations would truly protect the lives and property of its people by bringing peace and security in line with its commitments;

(g) However, as the United Nations, and in particular the Security Council, consistently failed to take effective action, the people’s faith and hope gradually faded;

(h) The Security Council had resolution (2669) on the Myanmar issue, but it had not been implemented;

(i) I had expressed my deep disappointment in the Myanmar people in November last year at the lack of effective action by the international community, and in particular the Security Council;

(j) I must reiterate this time, we are extremely helpless;

Statement by Ambassador Kyaw Moe Tun, Permanent Representative of the Republic of the Union of Myanmar to the United Nations, at the First Regular Session 2025 of the Executive Board of United Nations Children’s Fund, held on 4 February 2025

[Below Myanmar Text]

Press Release

At the first regular session of the Executive Board of the United Nations Children’s Fund for 2025, Ambassador Kyaw Moe Tun, Permanent Representative of the Republic of the Union of Myanmar to the United Nations, said: “Since the military attempted an illegal coup, children in Myanmar have been disproportionately affected by the atrocities committed by the military. The military has been targeting homes, schools, hospitals, refugee camps and other facilities with airstrikes increasing every year. In 2024 alone, the military carried out 1,315 airstrikes. Some children are forced to study in makeshift camps and bomb shelters. Children are being denied the vaccinations they need, and the lack of regular vaccinations can increase the spread of communicable diseases. In this situation, the only way to save the lives of innocent civilians, including children, is to end the military dictatorship and cut off the flow of jet fuel to the military. We urge the international community and UN agencies, including UNICEF, to support our efforts to cut off the flow of jet fuel to the military in order to save the lives of our people and our children (New York, 4 February 2025)

1. The first extraordinary regular session of the Executive Board of the United Nations Children’s Fund (UNICEF) for 2025 is being held at the United Nations Headquarters in New York from 4-2-2025 (Tuesday) to 6-2-2025 (Thursday).

2. The meeting opened at 1000 hours on 4-2-2025, with the opening remarks by the Permanent Representative of Tajikistan, Chair of the Executive Board, and Ms. Catherine Russell, Executive Director of UNICEF. The Executive Board Bureau members and observer countries/organizations then participated in a general discussion, and the Permanent Representative of Myanmar to the United Nations, Ambassador U Kyaw Moe Htun, also delivered a speech.

3. The key points of the speech delivered by the Permanent Representative of Myanmar included the following:

(a) We remain grateful to UNICEF for its work in protecting the rights of children, in line with its mandate, and for providing and facilitating access to essential services, particularly education and health services, to the most vulnerable people around the world;

(b) Our children in Myanmar depend on UNICEF and its partners for humanitarian assistance and life-saving support; Timely, adequate and manageable funding is essential to meet the growing humanitarian needs, but more needs to be done;

(c) Since the military attempted an illegal coup on 1 February 2021, children in Myanmar have been disproportionately affected by the atrocities committed by the military, and are facing a number of child-related crises, and today’s speech will focus on saving the lives of children;

(d) Six million children are facing the worst humanitarian crisis of all time, with 33 per cent of the estimated 3.5 million internally displaced persons (IDPs) being children;

(e) The indiscriminate bombing, killing and maiming by the military and its proxies have left many children dead, orphaned or disabled;

(f) The military’s airstrikes, particularly targeting homes, schools, hospitals, refugee camps and other facilities, have increased each year, reaching 1,315 airstrikes in 2024 alone, with some children being forced to study in makeshift tents and bomb shelters;

(g) Furthermore, childhood immunization is a serious problem in Myanmar, with many routine immunization programs in Myanmar, especially in hard-to-reach areas, being disrupted, leaving children deprived of the vaccines they need;

(h) The lack of regular vaccinations could lead to an increase in the spread of communicable diseases, which could have serious implications not only for the future of the country but also for neighbouring countries and the region;

(i) On the other hand, the physical and mental health and safety of children in Myanmar is unimaginable and poses a grave threat to their future;

(j) In this context, the only way to save the lives of innocent civilians, including children, is to end the military dictatorship and cut off the flow of aviation fuel to the military;

(k) According to our experts and sources, life-saving aid is currently not being delivered by air to hard-to-reach areas;

(l) Protecting children is key; We understand that this is the primary responsibility of the respective countries, but we also need international assistance. We therefore urge the international community and UN agencies, including UNICEF, to support our efforts to cut off the flow of jet fuel to the military in order to save the lives of our people and our children.

(m) We will continue to do our utmost to save the lives of children and their futures, and we appeal to you to save the lives of Burmese children and their futures.

4. The full text of the speech delivered by the Permanent Representative of Myanmar is attached (in English).

4 February 2025
Office of the Permanent Mission of Myanmar to the United Nations, New York City

