 Statement by Ambassador Kyaw Moe Tun, Permanent Representative of the Republic of the Union of Myanmar to the United Nations at the First Regular Session 2025 of the Executive Board of the United Nations Children's Fund (New York, 4 February 2025)

 Mr. President,

 At the outset, I wish to congratulate you Mr. President and other members of the Bureau for your election. I thank Madam Catherine Russell, Executive Director of UNICEF, for her statement.

 We always pay tribute to the UNICEF in discharging its mandate working to protect the rights of every child and to provide and advocate for required assistance especially in the education and health services to the most vulnerable ones around the globe.

 In Myanmar, our children rely on the humanitarian and life-saving aid from UNICEF and its partners. Timely, sufficient and flexible funding is critical to meet the growing humanitarian needs. However, I must emphasize that much more needs to be done.

(e) The 2018 review of the Committee on Development Policy (CDP) found that Myanmar met all three criteria for graduating from Least Developed Country status for the first time, but that the country had suffered a multifaceted deterioration in economic, financial and social aspects due to the attempted illegal military coup in 2021;
(f) According to a UNDP report, almost half of Myanmar’s population now lives below the country’s poverty line, the middle class is on the verge of disappearing, and urban poverty rates are rising; 3.6 million people are internally displaced and nearly 22 million are in need of humanitarian assistance;
(g) Lack of employment opportunities, inadequate wages, and forced military service, particularly targeting young men, are driving the exodus of young people;
(h) According to the World Bank, the lack of formal employment opportunities, the exodus of skilled workers, the depletion of human resources, and the destruction of education and health services threaten Myanmar’s long-term development and poverty reduction potential.
(i) In addition, the earthquakes that struck in March this year have exacerbated the suffering and livelihoods of people in the affected areas. Even as the natural disasters continue, the terrorist group has continued to carry out airstrikes targeting civilians and has blocked humanitarian access to the earthquake-affected areas.
(j) Despite these challenges, the Government of National Unity is providing essential public services, including humanitarian and life-saving assistance, to those affected by both man-made and natural disasters. The National Unity Government stands ready to work with the international community to increase humanitarian assistance in line with the principle of leaving no one behind;
(k) The benefits of development are fragile and fragile, hard-won and easily lost;
(l) Development cannot be sustainable without peace and stability in Myanmar;
(m) Cooperation cannot be mutually beneficial if it does not benefit our people;
(n) Investments and infrastructure projects without local support cannot be sustainable; and economic recovery cannot be achieved without restoring democracy and people-centered governance;
(o) It is abundantly clear that the fraudulent and fraudulent elections to be held by the terrorist military regime will not bring peace and stability for long-term benefits, mutual cooperation and sustainable development; The sham election will be a license for the military to commit further atrocities against the Burmese people, and the people are resisting the military regime’s deceitful process in every possible way.
(a) We therefore urge the international community to support the people of Burma, who are firmly determined to end the military dictatorship and build a federal democratic union for justice, peace, stability and sustainable development.
